+# 2026-11-11 Does Node 22 `fetch()` honour `HTTPS_PROXY` by default?
+
+## Answer
+
+**No.** In Node 22, global `fetch()` ignores `HTTP_PROXY` / `HTTPS_PROXY` / `NO_PROXY`
+unless you opt in. Since **v22.21.0** you can opt in with either:
+
+- the environment variable `NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY=1`, or
+- the CLI flag `--use-env-proxy` (wins if both are set).
+
+Releases before v22.21.0 have no built-in switch. There you have to set a dispatcher
+yourself, e.g. undici's `EnvHttpProxyAgent` or `ProxyAgent` via `setGlobalDispatcher`.
+
+## Evidence
+
+### 1. Node's documentation (v22 CLI docs, https://nodejs.org/docs/latest-v22.x/api/cli.html)
+
+> #### `NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY=1`
+> Added in: v22.21.0 — Stability: 1.1 - Active Development
+>
+> When enabled, Node.js parses the `HTTP_PROXY`, `HTTPS_PROXY` and `NO_PROXY` environment
+> variables during startup, and tunnels requests over the specified proxy.
+>
+> This can also be enabled using the `--use-env-proxy` command-line flag. When both are
+> set, `--use-env-proxy` takes precedence.
+
+`--use-env-proxy` has the same "Added in: v22.21.0" and the same Stability 1.1 marking.
+The phrase "when enabled" means the behaviour is off by default.
+
+The man page shipped with the installed Node (`/opt/node22/share/man/man1/node.1`) says the
+same, and adds: *"This currently only affects requests sent over `fetch()`. Support for
+other built-in http and https methods is under way."*
+
+### 2. Empirical test (Node v22.23.4, this machine)
+
+A local HTTP proxy logged every request it received. Child processes ran
+`fetch('https://example.invalid/')` with `HTTPS_PROXY`/`HTTP_PROXY` pointing at that proxy:
+
+| Mode | Proxy received | Child result |
+|---|---|---|
+| default (env var only) | nothing | `ENOTFOUND`: fetch went straight to DNS |
+| `NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY=1` | `CONNECT example.invalid:443` | went through the proxy |
+| `--use-env-proxy` | `CONNECT example.invalid:443` | went through the proxy |
+
+In both opt-in modes Node also printed
+`[UNDICI-EHPA] Warning: EnvHttpProxyAgent is experimental`. This shows the opt-in is
+built on undici's `EnvHttpProxyAgent`.
+
+## Caveats
+
+- The feature is marked **Stability 1.1 (Active Development)** and prints an experimental
+  warning, so its behaviour may still change.
+- In the 22.x docs it applies to `fetch()` only. Under this switch, `http`/`https`
+  `request()` and `get()` are not routed through the proxy.
+- On Node 22 < 22.21.0 (and on Node 18/20), you need code to use a proxy:
+  ```js
+  import { setGlobalDispatcher, EnvHttpProxyAgent } from 'undici';
+  setGlobalDispatcher(new EnvHttpProxyAgent());
+  ```
